[/Thinking]

Here is how you can achieve this. I will provide a standard **HTML/CSS/JS** solution, as well as a **React** solution (since you used the camelCase `onClick`, which is very common in React).

The best practice is to wrap your input and a `<button>` inside a container. You then use CSS `position: absolute` to put the icon over the right side of the input.

### Option 1: React (Tailwind or standard CSS)

Here is a clean React component handling the state and the `onClick` event.

```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import './Search.css'; // See CSS below

export default function SearchInput() {
  const [inputValue, setInputValue] = useState('');

  // This is the function called when the icon is clicked
  const handleSearch = () => {
    if (!inputValue) return; // Prevent empty searches
    console.log("You searched for:", inputValue);
    // Add your API call or logic here
  };

  // Optional: Allow user to press "Enter" to search
  const handleKeyDown = (e) => {
    if (e.key === 'Enter') {
      handleSearch();
    }
  };

  return (
    <div className="input-wrapper">
      <input
        type="text"
        placeholder="Search..."
        value={inputValue}
        onChange={(e) => setInputValue(e.target.value)}
        onKeyDown={handleKeyDown}
        className="search-input"
      />
      {/* Wrap the SVG in a button for accessibility and clickability */}
      <button onClick={handleSearch} className="search-button" aria-label="Search">
        {/* Reading Glass / Search SVG */}
        <svg 
          xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" 
          width="20" 
          height="20" 
          viewBox="0 0 24 24" 
          fill="none" 
          stroke="currentColor" 
          strokeWidth="2" 
          strokeLinecap="round" 
          strokeLinejoin="round"
        >
          <circle cx="11" cy="11" r="8"></circle>
          <line x1="21" y1="21" x2="16.65" y2="16.65"></line>
        </svg>
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Option 2: Pure HTML / JavaScript

If you are just using vanilla HTML and JavaScript, here is the basic structure.

**HTML:**
```html
<div class="input-wrapper">
  <input type="text" id="myInput" class="search-input" placeholder="Search..." />
  <button id="searchBtn" class="search-button">
    <!-- SVG Icon -->
    <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
      <circle cx="11" cy="11" r="8"></circle>
      <line x1="21" y1="21" x2="16.65" y2="16.65"></line>
    </svg>
  </button>
</div>
```

**JavaScript:**
```javascript
const inputElement = document.getElementById('myInput');
const searchButton = document.getElementById('searchBtn');

function performSearch() {
  const value = inputElement.value;
  console.log("Searching for:", value);
  // Do your logic here
}

searchButton.addEventListener('click', performSearch);
```

### The Required CSS (Works for both React and HTML)

This CSS ensures the icon sits securely inside the right side of the input, and that the text won't type *underneath* the icon.

```css
/* Container must be relative */
.input-wrapper {
  position: relative;
  display: inline-block;
  width: 300px; /* Set to whatever width you need */
}

/* The input needs extra padding on the right side */
.search-input {
  width: 100%;
  padding: 10px 40px 10px 12px; /* Top, Right(extra), Bottom, Left */
  font-size: 16px;
  border: 1px solid #ccc;
  border-radius: 4px;
  box-sizing: border-box;
}

/* Position the button absolutely on the right */
.search-button {
  position: absolute;
  right: 8px; /* Distance from the right edge */
  top: 50%;
  transform: translateY(-50%); /* Perfectly centers it vertically */
  background: transparent;
  border: none;
  cursor: pointer;
  padding: 0;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  color: #555; /* Icon color */
}

.search-button:hover {
  color: #000; /* Darkens icon on hover */
}
```

### Why do it this way?
1. **`position: relative` on the wrapper** allows you to use `position: absolute` on the button to stick it inside the input area.
2. **`padding-right: 40px` on the input** prevents the user's text from hiding behind the magnifying glass when they type a long sentence.
3. **Wrapping the SVG in a `<button>`** prevents issues with clicking directly on SVG paths and allows users traversing with a keyboard (Tab key) to select and press it easily.

